# TMS Working Memory

Last reconciled: 2026-09-11
Repository: `https://github.com/lucazsoft/tution-Management-System/`
Branch: `ui_enhance`
Repository baseline: `a39b2ff Merge pull request #1 from lucazsoft/staging`
Worktree at reconciliation: teacher daily-progress persistence and UI changes in progress

## Teacher daily progress integration (2026-09-11)

- Product rule corrected: a daily chapter/topic log is immutable after its
  first publication for that topic and date. The UI presents the saved entry
  as locked and the API returns 409 for duplicate same-day publication. A new
  record can be created automatically on the next date.
- A follow-up compatibility fix handles rolling deployments where the web app
  reaches an older API instance that returns only `{ message, log }`. The UI
  now derives confirmation from `topic.status` when available and safely falls
  back to the persisted log/draft status and client timestamp, preventing the
  `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'status')` failure.
- The Teacher portal Daily Update Log now keeps the selected topic visible after
  saving instead of automatically jumping to the next incomplete topic. This
  makes the persisted completion state immediately visible.
- Selecting a topic with an update for the current day restores its saved note
  and status in a disabled, read-only published state. The next calendar day
  naturally has no topic/date record and therefore opens a fresh publish form.
- Save controls remain disabled while the request is in flight. Successful
  saves show an accessible inline confirmation with the authoritative status
  and server timestamp, as well as the existing toast. Class changes select the
  appropriate active/upcoming topic without interfering with post-save state.
- `POST /api/teacher/syllabus/:syllabusId/topic-log` continues to enforce
  teacher, class, tenant, syllabus, and topic ownership. Its transaction now
  returns the persisted log, topic status, and recalculated chapter status, and
  invalid dates return 400 instead of falling through as server errors.
- Progress is production-persisted in `TopicProgressLog` with the existing
  `(topicId, logDate)` uniqueness constraint and synchronized to `SyllabusTopic` and
  `SyllabusChapter`; students and Branch Admins consume those same records.
- Verification on 2026-09-11: API `npm run build` passed after regenerating the
  current Prisma client and installing declared dependencies. Web
  `npm run build` passed with the existing 586 KiB entrypoint-size warning.
  Web `npm run lint` passed with pre-existing warnings and no new Daily Update
  Log warning. `git diff --check` passed apart from line-ending notices.

## Deployment hardening (2026-08-28)

- Production and staging are separate Coolify applications tracking `main` and
  `staging`, respectively. The required team promotion workflow and GitHub
  branch-protection settings are documented in
  `docs/DEPLOYMENT_WORKFLOW.md`.
- Hosted Compose no longer publishes PostgreSQL, API, or web ports on the VPS.
  It exposes container-network ports for Coolify routing; local host mappings
  live in `compose.override.yml`.
- API and web builds now use Node 22 consistently with CI. The API runtime runs
  as the unprivileged `node` user.
- Production SMS is fail-closed: `DISABLED` returns a delivery failure,
  `AAKASH` requires `AAKASH_SMS_AUTH_TOKEN`, and `MOCK` remains forbidden in
  production. Compose forwards the Aakash token to the API.
- CI now has least-privilege permissions, concurrency cancellation, timeouts,
  all focused API unit/configuration checks, a high-severity production audit,
  production Compose validation, and API/web Docker image builds.
- The 2026-08-28 Coolify failure was caused by `NODE_ENV=production` together
  with `SMS_PROVIDER=MOCK`; database startup and Prisma migration had passed.
- Production secrets disclosed during setup must be rotated. PostgreSQL
  rotation must also update the initialized database role (or safely replace
  the empty new volume), not only the Coolify environment value.
- The production login connectivity defect was traced to the Better Auth web
  client reading `VITE_API_ORIGIN` even though the web app uses Webpack. Hosted
  builds therefore fell back to the visitor's `http://localhost:3001` while
  ordinary API calls correctly used `TMS_API_BASE_URL`. The auth client now
  derives its origin from the same injected API URL, CI rejects localhost URLs
  in the production bundle, and login distinguishes service connectivity
  failures from invalid credentials without consuming a credential attempt.
- API CORS already allows the single configured `WEB_ORIGIN` with credentials,
  Better Auth already uses that origin as `trustedOrigins`, and auth fetches
  already use `credentials: include`. The API-host-only session cookie is the
  correct scope: both HTTPS subdomains are same-site, and the web host does not
  need access to the HTTP-only API cookie. No nginx API proxy or shared parent-
  domain cookie is required.

