## Recently incorporated security work

- Server-enforced email OTP two-factor authentication now uses the Better Auth
  two-factor plugin. A password sign-in for a `twoFactorEnabled` user creates
  only a short-lived challenge; there is no authenticated session until OTP
  verification succeeds. The web client uses the plugin's challenge, OTP,
  verification, and server-issued trusted-device cookies rather than
  client-side authorization state.
- Migrations `20260802090000_better_auth_two_factor` and
  `20260802100000_backfill_email_otp_two_factor` add/backfill the
  plugin-managed 2FA record for legacy users. The seed flow also creates or
  removes that record consistently with `SEED_ADMIN_2FA`.
- The live Compose validation on 2026-08-02 passed the complete 2FA path:
  password sign-in returned a two-factor challenge, the pending challenge was
  denied a protected route with `401`, a correct mock OTP created the session,
  and that session read the authorized provisioning queue. The isolated
  Tenant Admin integration test also passes against PostgreSQL on local port
  `5433`.
- Compose/runtime configuration now distinguishes local development from
  production: production requires explicit secrets and HTTPS auth/web origins,
  rejects the development provisioning surface and mock SMS, and local Compose
  forwards the documented `SEED_ADMIN_*` variables so `npm run db:seed` works
  normally. `npm run test:runtime-config`, API build, Compose validation, and
  the local seed command pass.
- Authentication throttling and monitoring are now server-side and persistent.
  Migration `20260802110000_auth_rate_limit_and_monitoring` adds Better Auth's
  PostgreSQL-backed `RateLimit` counter and a privacy-preserving
  `AuthSecurityEvent` record. Credential sign-in, Better Auth 2FA, and the
  existing password-reset endpoints allow five requests per fifteen minutes
  before returning `429` with `X-Retry-After`; failed/rate-limited credential
  sign-ins are recorded without passwords, emails, or raw IP addresses. The
  Tenant Admin integration test verifies the login and password-reset limits,
  the monitoring event, and the rest of the authorization flow.
- Client IP forwarding is deliberately not trusted until deployment provides a
  configured reverse proxy. Until then the limiter uses Better Auth's safe
  shared bucket rather than accepting a spoofable forwarding header. Configure
  the actual trusted proxy before an Internet-facing production deployment.
- API responses now receive browser hardening headers (`Content-Security-Policy`
  for `/api`, frame denial, no-sniff, referrer and permissions policy, and a
  correlation ID); production additionally emits HSTS. Every API `5xx` JSON
  response is replaced with a generic `Internal Server Error` plus that
  correlation ID, including legacy route handlers that previously returned
  `error.message`. Internal details are logged server-side only. The Tenant
  Admin integration test verifies headers and the no-detail error boundary.

